did did somebody I mean do you think  other events in the past like you know  the scene with Weaver on what happens in  Waco where you're going back and forth  and you you bring up BOSU ball has some  kind of a trust after the shooter killed  his wife you know were there other  experiences you you had that led you to  being prepared for the negotiating with  David Koresh and if yes what were some  of those well yeah I worked a lot of  cases before the 1993 Waco incident so  you know the FBI's whole curriculum in  terms of negotiations was you know it's  part art and part science I mean the  science is to get people to cooperate  which is our goal we need to establish a  relationship a trust so that's the  scientific part we we know the  requirement and the goal the other part  is a bit of art how do you do that how  do you project your sincerity and  honesty and genuine people like to work  with people that they like and respect  so I mean you get what you give so you  know those are sort of the parameters so  when we dealt with the David Koresh we  knew we had responded to an incident  that resulted in a terrible shootout  between the ATF a different agency and  the Branch Davidians the Koresh LED and  we knew that there were dead people  there was wounded people there was great  emotion there was you know a great bit  of anger and frustration one side versus  the other we had to we the FBI  negotiation team we had to come in and  sort of separate ourselves and try to  project that we were sort of a neutral a  mediating entity you know that hey we're  not the ATF and we don't pretend to  understand all the issues that are  important to you but we want to learn we  want to see if we can work with you so  that we can avoid further bloodshed and  we can get this resolved peacefully and  that's what we did the first half of the  Waco incident we got 35 people out  including 21 children a fact that's  often overlooked and I'm very proud of  that it's unfortunate that that thing's  digress  you know after I left the scene but  yeah that's that's kind of our goal so  yeah I think we were well prepared to  deal with the David Koresh but we had  other internal disagreements in the FBI  that I'm sure we'll talk about in this  interview and certainly discussed in my  book I guess the biggest thing is you  know prior to a negotiating with David  Koresh who was did you have a scenario  like one that's you know like for  example you talked to a Michael Jordan  or you talked to a Adelaide Kobe Bryant  or you talked to a Brady or some of  these guys in sports they'll sell you'll  say so tell me the toughest opponent you  ever faced and yes well you know I hated  playing against you know Joe Dumars I  hated defend against this guy who was  the toughest person you faced off pree  David Koresh that you were both you know  excited with the results that you got or  one where you said I wish I wouldn't  take him that approach in a negotiation  were there any on both sides that you  remember  yeah there's two that stand out and I  talked about both in my book one was a  case in the 80s in Virginia where a guy  kidnapped his former common-law wife and  child and from Connecticut and drove  them to Virginian we located them in an  in a remote farmhouse and and I ended up  negotiating with him for some ten hours  now he was a very dangerous guy there  were times where we communicated quite  effectively but his controlling nature  over his former ex-wife and child made  him a high potential for lethality we  were pretty certain he was going to kill  her and possibly himself in the child  suicide often follows a homicidal act so  it was a huge challenge connecting with  him so he was one of the toughest cases  I've ever worked with and in fact we  ended up I had to negotiate him out of  the house on his hopes of getting to a  helicopter where an FBI marksman took  his life the other case was also in the  80s and was on a train in in Raleigh  North Carolina an Amtrak train a guy was  a drug runner and he was traveling with  his sister and her two small children  they were sort of his disguise and he  end up having an argument with her and  shooting and killing her the train was  the cars were detached in Raleigh and  negotiations set forth this was  extremely challenging because he only  spoke Spanish he he was one of those who  was almost afraid to communicate with us  for fear that we would hear the fear in  his voice that we would psychologically  get into his head or whatever it was and  at the same time we had children dying  of dehydration and one of them did die  so we had that clock ticking against us  on the one hand and on the other hand we  had this challenge of of an effective  communication channel you know it  ultimately worked out that he did  surrender we saved one of the two  children but it was an extremely  difficult case he subsequently died in  prison but those two standout I think in  the area you talked about Patrick as  being extraordinarily challenging ones  that I dealt with you know well prior to  Waco  [Music]
 

